# Development Workflow (How-to)

This guide covers the most common maintenance tasks.

## Use the pinned Python

The generated `.python-version` records the exact Python interpreter that ran
Copier. `uv` uses this file automatically, and template updates preserve it.

## Run local QA before pushing

```bash
make qa
make test
```

CI skips Python linting and tests for Markdown-only changes. Workflow linting runs only when files under `.github/workflows/` change.

If `prek` is not installed, install it once with:

```bash
uv tool install prek
```

## Update dependencies safely

This template uses `uv` groups and cooldown windows.
Dependabot checks Python dependencies (including all `uv` groups and the build
backend) and GitHub Actions every Monday at a random time assigned by GitHub.
No fixed `time` is configured, so generated repositories do not share a forced
start time. Minor and patch version
updates are grouped per ecosystem; major updates remain separate. Security
updates use separate groups and are triggered independently of that schedule.
All updates target this repository's default branch.

New repositories created through `gh` automatically enable Dependabot alerts,
the dependency graph, and security updates. To enable these settings on an
existing repository or retry setup, authenticate `gh` with repository admin
access and run:

```bash
make dependabot
```

The command is idempotent and reports permission or policy failures. Fine-grained
tokens need repository **Administration: write**. You can also opt in during a
template update with `uvx python-package-copier-template --configure-dependabot .`
or `copier update --trust --data gh_dependabot_configure=true .`; this choice is
not saved for future updates. Commit and push `.github/dependabot.yml` to the
default branch to activate scheduled version updates.

After dependency changes:

```bash
uv sync
make qa
make test
```

Commit `uv.lock` for reproducible development and CI, even for a library; it does
not constrain dependencies for consumers of the published package. The local
`uv-lock` prek hook runs `uv lock` when `pyproject.toml`, `uv.toml`, or `uv.lock`
changes. If it updates the lockfile, review and stage the changes before retrying
the commit. CI checks `uv lock --check` directly in the existing QA job, before
running project commands; prek is not needed for that check. The template-update
workflow regenerates the lockfile after Copier and includes it in its PR.

## Protect main

New GitHub repositories created by the template automatically receive a
`python-package-template-main` branch ruleset after the initial push. It requires
PRs into `main` even for administrators, blocks force pushes and deletion, and
requires review conversations to be resolved. No approval is required, so solo
maintainers can merge their own PRs. The baseline does not require specific CI
checks because some jobs are conditional; existing stricter rules still apply.
Tags and `release/*` branches are not restricted, and releases have no bypass.

Reusing or updating an existing repository leaves GitHub protection unchanged
unless you opt in with `uvx python-package-copier-template --configure-main-protection .`
or `copier update --trust --data gh_main_protection_configure=true .`.
This choice is not saved for future updates. To configure protection separately
or retry failed setup, run:

```bash
make protect-main
```

The command resolves the current repository with `gh repo view`. You can also
run `sh .github/configure-main-protection.sh OWNER/REPO`. Repeated runs update
only the named ruleset, resetting its manual edits to this baseline; other
rulesets and classic branch protection are preserved.

Authenticate `gh` with repository admin access; fine-grained tokens need
**Administration: write**. Rulesets are available for public repositories on
Free plans; private repositories need an eligible paid plan. Organization
policies may also restrict setup. Automatic setup warns and continues on
failure; explicit updates and `make protect-main` fail visibly. See
[GitHub's rulesets documentation](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-rulesets/about-rulesets).

## Prepare a release

Start from a clean, up-to-date `main` and run the project checks first. Then prepare a version-bump PR:

```bash
make qa
make release-pr BUMP=minor
```

`BUMP` accepts `patch`, `minor`, or `major` and defaults to `minor`. The target
creates a `release/<version>` branch and opens a PR. GitHub creates a draft release
while the PR is open; subsequent pushes update its target without overwriting
edited notes. Review those notes before merging. Do not publish the draft manually
before merge: the final tag must point to the integrated commit, not the PR head.

Merging the PR publishes the draft at the integrated commit and dispatches
`cd.yml` on its tag with `publish=true`, starting PyPI publishing. This explicit
dispatch uses the existing {term}`GITHUB_TOKEN` and avoids relying on another
workflow being triggered by a bot-generated release event. No extra secret is
required. CD validates that the published tag is already integrated into `main`
before building. Normal manual dispatches without `publish=true` remain
documentation-only.

The workflow refuses a missing draft or a tag pointing to another commit. If CD
dispatch fails after publication, rerun the release workflow to retry the dispatch;
already-published releases are accepted only when their tag matches the integrated
commit. Closing an unmerged PR does not publish anything.

### Verify release attestations

Each wheel and source distribution has a PEP 740 publish attestation signed
through Sigstore and uploaded to PyPI with the package.

Verify a published artifact by passing its PyPI file URL:

```bash
uvx pypi-attestations verify pypi \
  --repository https://github.com/{{ repository_namespace }}/{{ repository_name }} \
  https://files.pythonhosted.org/path/to/distribution.whl
```

## Preview documentation in pull requests

When docs files change in a PR, CI deploys a preview to:

```text
https://{{ repository_namespace }}.github.io/{{ repository_name }}/_preview/pr-<PR_NUMBER>/
```

If needed, dispatch docs publishing manually:

```bash
gh workflow run cd.yml --ref main
```

`gh` authentication can use {term}`GH_TOKEN`.
